Title:
MostShittyEDR — The World’s Most Intentionally Terrible Endpoint Detection & Response Agent

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
MostShittyEDR is an intentionally weak, educational Windows EDR agent written in Nim, optionally paired with a kernel driver, designed to teach detection mechanics and practical bypass techniques. It implements 9 simplified “realistic” detection rules (process/command-line matching, LSASS dump heuristics, PowerShell flags, SHA256 signatures, static import hook detection, ETW integrity checks, and basic PE structure analysis) and maps them to 42 bypass challenges with solutions. The tool supports two modes: user-mode polling via Toolhelp32 snapshots and kernel-mode eventing via driver callbacks with IOCTL-based communication. The kernel driver adds process/thread notify callbacks, LSASS handle protection via ObRegisterCallbacks, and kernel-level blocking/termination, but also intentionally exposes weaknesses (e.g., unauthenticated device IOCTL access and event-slot DoS). It’s useful for red teamers, pentesters, malware devs, and defenders who want a controlled lab to understand how EDR telemetry and enforcement can be evaded or abused. Its value is in being a “CTF-style” EDR sandbox that demonstrates both common EDR design patterns and common implementation pitfalls.

Technical Focus:
- Windows user-mode process monitoring (Toolhelp32 snapshots) vs kernel callbacks
- Windows kernel driver telemetry/enforcement (PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks)
- IOCTL device communication design and abuse scenarios
- ETW provider/session usage and tamper/integrity checks
- Static detection approaches: string/keyword rules, SHA256 signatures, PE/import analysis
- EDR evasion primitives: renaming, cmdline obfuscation, dynamic API resolution/direct syscalls, ETW patching

Use Cases:
- Build a lab to practice EDR bypass techniques against common rule types
- Demonstrate why polling-based monitoring creates timing gaps and blind spots
- Study kernel-driver EDR architecture (callbacks, handle filtering, pre-exec blocking)
- Test offensive techniques like ETW blinding, import-hiding, and signature evasion
- Teach defenders how weak rule logic and poor driver ACLs create bypass/abuse paths

Keywords:
Windows EDR, Nim, kernel driver, WDK, IOCTL, \\\\.\\MostShittyEDR, PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutineEx, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, ObRegisterCallbacks, LSASS, handle protection, ZwTerminateProcess, Toolhelp32Snapshot, ETW, EtwEventWrite, process blocking, command-line obfuscation, direct syscalls, import table analysis, BYOVD